<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The adaptive icon: a background colour and a foreground bitmap, which Android
  26+ composes and masks into whatever shape the launcher uses. A single flat PNG
  would be cropped differently on every device, and this app's mark is a shape
  that has to survive that.

  The mark is the owner-supplied gauge: a purple-to-blue speedometer ring with a
  needle. It arrived as a 96x96 JPEG rather than as vector paths, so the
  foreground is a density-specific raster (`drawable-*/ic_launcher_foreground.png`)
  and not a `<vector>`. There is no outline here to re-draw at scale — only
  pixels to place inside the 72dp safe zone — so upscaling beyond the source is
  soft, which is the cost of using the artwork as supplied.

  The background is white because the artwork was drawn on white. A coloured
  plate behind a white-backed bitmap would show as a coloured border around the
  mark, so the plate and the artwork's own field are the same colour on purpose.

  There is deliberately no `<monochrome>` layer. That layer has to be a solid
  silhouette the system can tint, and a colour raster is not one; pointing it at
  this drawable would make themed icons render the gauge itself as the mask.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground" />
</adaptive-icon>
